schurRing(Ring) -- The Schur ring corresponding to a given Symmetric ring.
**************************************************************************

  * Function: *note schurRing: schurRing,
  * Usage: 
        S = schurRing R
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Basis: Basis, => ..., default value "Schur", Specifies the basis
        to use for a Schur ring
      * *note EHPVariables: EHPVariables, => ..., default value (e,h,p),
        Specifies sequence of symbols representing e-, h-, and p-functions
      * *note GroupActing: GroupActing, => ..., default value "GL", Specifies
        the group that is acting
      * *note OddOrEven: OddOrEven, => ..., default value null, Select type
        $B_n$ or type $D_n$ for an orthogonal Schur ring
      * *note SVariable: SVariable, => ..., default value s, Specifies symbol
        representing s-functions
  * Outputs:
      * S, a *note Schur ring: SchurRing,, 

Description
===========



Given a ring R, the function schurRing attempts to return a Schur ring S that
is associated to R in a natural way. Namely, if the attribute R.Schur points to
a Schur ring, then the function returns that ring. If R is already a Schur
ring, then the ring R is returned. Otherwise, if the Schur level of R is at
least one, then the function constructs a Schur ring over the base ring A of R,
having the same relative dimension over A as R. If the Schur level of R is
zero, then an error is returned.



+-----------------------------+
|i1 : R = schurRing(QQ,r,6);  |
+-----------------------------+
|i2 : schurRing R             |
|                             |
|o2 = R                       |
|                             |
|o2 : SchurRing               |
+-----------------------------+
|i3 : Q = symmetricRing(QQ,3);|
+-----------------------------+
|i4 : A = schurRing Q;        |
+-----------------------------+
|i5 : schurRing Q             |
|                             |
|o5 = A                       |
|                             |
|o5 : SchurRing               |
+-----------------------------+



Passing an existing Schur ring to schurRing simply returns it, which is
convenient as a guard when a function wants to accept either a Symmetric ring
or a Schur ring as input.



+---------------------------+
|i6 : S = schurRing(QQ,s,4);|
+---------------------------+
|i7 : schurRing S === S     |
|                           |
|o7 = true                  |
+---------------------------+



For a Symmetric ring with the "Sn" interpretation, the associated Schur ring
inherits this flavor and is cached on the ring.



+---------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : Rsn = symmetricRing(QQ,4,GroupActing => "Sn");|
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : Ssn = schurRing Rsn;                          |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : numgens Ssn                                  |
|                                                   |
|o10 = 4                                            |
+---------------------------------------------------+



The construction also works over polynomial coefficient rings, producing a
Schur ring with the same parameters as coefficients.



+----------------------------------+
|i11 : Rx = symmetricRing(QQ[x],3);|
+----------------------------------+
|i12 : Sx = schurRing Rx;          |
+----------------------------------+
|i13 : coefficientRing Sx          |
|                                  |
|o13 = QQ[x]                       |
|                                  |
|o13 : PolynomialRing              |
+----------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note symmetricRing: symmetricRing, -- Make a Symmetric ring
  * *note SchurRing: SchurRing, -- The class of all Schur rings
  * *note schurRing: schurRing, -- Make a SchurRing

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note schurRing(Ring): schurRing_lpRing_rp, -- The Schur ring corresponding
